Notes for Testing
• Set header Content-Type: application/json for POST and PUT.
• After GET /api/employees, visually confirm in the Postman response body that the JSON objects contain
only empId, empName, and department — no salary, no password.
• Open the H2 console and inspect the EMPLOYEES table directly: the PASSWORD column exists in the
database even though it never appears in any API response — this is the DTO pattern working as intended.
• Save all four requests into the Postman Collection for reuse.
How to Run
1. Place the files in the package structure shown above.
2. Run mvn spring-boot:run (or run EmployeeApiApplication from an IDE).
3. The API is available at http://localhost:8080/api/employees.
4. Open http://localhost:8080/h2-console and connect with JDBC URL
jdbc:h2:mem:employeedb, username sa, empty password.
5. Execute the Postman requests in order: POST → GET (confirm no password field) → PUT → DELETE.
